codex
**Verdict: REJECT**

1. **High — `pop sp` bypasses stack-pointer write rejection.**  
   [stack_bound.py:94](scripts/checks/lib/stack_bound.py:94) returns before checking the destination. The whitelist admits `pop sp`, but this instruction increments RSP by two and overwrites its low 16 bits; the model merely subtracts eight from depth. Confirmed reproducer: `push rax; pop sp; ret` is accepted with `bound 8`. Reject stack-pointer destinations before handling `pop`, and validate operand widths.

2. **High — partial frame-pointer writes preserve a stale bound.**  
   [stack_bound.py:111](scripts/checks/lib/stack_bound.py:111) invalidates only `rbp`/`ebp`; the whitelist also admits instructions writing `bp`/`bpl`. Confirmed: `push rbp; mov rbp,rsp; mov bp,0; mov eax,[rbp-0x100]; pop rbp; ret` is accepted with `bound 264`, although clearing BP can move the addressed location tens of kilobytes lower. Invalidate or refuse every frame-register alias.

3. **Medium — capped producers can exhaust uncapped shell consumers.**  
   [slice0_artifacts.sh:30](scripts/checks/slice0_artifacts.sh:30) removes the inherited cap, while [slice0_common.sh:57](scripts/checks/lib/slice0_common.sh:57) captures the emitter’s entire stdout into an uncapped Bash variable. A producer can stream arbitrarily large output while remaining below its own memory cap; the receiving shell grows without a bound. Other command substitutions have the same exposure. Bound consumer processes or enforce output-size limits before loading output into variables.

Round-1 findings **1–4 and 6 are resolved in the inspected code**. Finding **5’s soft-limit escape is fixed**, but its replacement leaves the consumer-memory gap above.

Validation used read-only inspection and in-memory analyzer reproducers; I did not rerun the CI gates. No files were modified.
